rome (e.g., optic neuritis, demyelination): nonfocal exam and lack of focal deficits argue against this as the dominant mechanism", "Systemic infection with neurological complications: fever and constitutional symptoms could align with this, but blast count strongly suggests hematological origin"], "alarm_features": ["Constitutional symptoms (fever, weight loss, night sweats)", "Significant leukocytosis with blasts (57,500/mm³ with 35% blasts)", "New neurological deficits (visual acuity decline, ataxia)"]}, "other_mass": 0.0, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No acute life-threatening instability markers (airway, respiratory, hemodynamic, or neurological compromise requiring immediate action). Blasts in peripheral blood suggest acute leukemia but do not require stabilization before diagnostic clarification.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Root posterior threshold is 0.75 but dangerous alternative B1 (posterior 0.1447, danger 0.8) remains unresolved. Further differentiation between myeloid (B1.1 AML) and chronic myeloid (B3.1 CML-BC) processes is required despite high blast count."}, "turn_budget_used": 3, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.55, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.10167689130975999, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Lymphoma', 'Myeloproliferative disorder', 'elevated white blood cell count', 'Mediastinal lymphadenopathy', 'elevated red blood cell count', 'blood', 'leukocytosis'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "root_revision_needed": true, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B2", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.465}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B3.1", "label":